1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,960
Your documentation says one thing.

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:05,872
Your code does another.

3
00:00:05,872 --> 00:00:09,000
Your AI agent reads the documentation and trusts it.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,012
Even your agent instructions go out of date.

5
00:00:13,012 --> 00:00:15,331
The agent writes the wrong code.

6
00:00:15,331 --> 00:00:18,733
You find the problem in review, or in production.

7
00:00:19,533 --> 00:00:23,163
DocGuard compares your documentation with your code.

8
00:00:23,163 --> 00:00:26,457
It finds each claim that is no longer true.

9
00:00:26,457 --> 00:00:28,867
It shows you the file and the line.

10
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:31,649
Run one command.

11
00:00:31,649 --> 00:00:36,498
More than thirty checks read your routes, schemas, tests, and specs.

12
00:00:36,498 --> 00:00:40,326
DocGuard drafts missing documentation from your code.

13
00:00:40,326 --> 00:00:43,233
It stops the merge when an important check fails.

14
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:47,315
Your agent can also ask DocGuard directly.

15
00:00:47,315 --> 00:00:52,100
The MCP server gives the agent the current documentation before it writes code.

16
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:56,426
DocGuard runs on your machine and sends nothing out.

17
00:00:56,426 --> 00:01:02,878
It works with Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Spec Kit, and GitHub Actions.

18
00:01:02,878 --> 00:01:06,567
It reads English, Portuguese, and Spanish.

19
00:01:07,367 --> 00:01:10,011
DocGuard is free and open source.

20
00:01:10,011 --> 00:01:14,163
Start in one minute, and star the repository on GitHub.

21
00:01:14,163 --> 00:01:18,066
Keep your documentation true, so your agents stay correct.

